<BaseLayout
  title="Nominal Pipe Size: DN, NPS and A Explained — HowNote"
  description="Understand why DN, NPS and regional A labels must be kept separate from measured outside and inside diameter."
>
  <section class="hero container legal-hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">Methodology · Pipe Size</div>
    <h1>Nominal pipe size is a reference—not a ruler reading.</h1>
    <p>HowNote separates the designation used to identify a pipe from the dimensions used to calculate, inspect and order it.</p>
  </section>

  <article class="container prose">
    <h2>DN</h2>
    <p>DN is a nominal designation used to identify a compatible size family. The number should not be treated as a measured outside or inside diameter in millimeters.</p>

    <h2>NPS</h2>
    <p>NPS is a nominal pipe-size designation. An NPS number is not converted directly by multiplying it by 25.4 to obtain the actual pipe OD.</p>

    <h2>A designation</h2>
    <p>Labels such as 50A are commonly used in parts of Asia as regional convenience aliases. HowNote displays them separately and does not describe them as ASME terminology.</p>

    <h2>Actual dimensions</h2>
    <p>Weight, clearance, flow area and inspection calculations require actual geometry such as OD, wall thickness and ID. A complete purchase specification may also require schedule, dimensional standard, product standard and edition/year.</p>

    <h2>How HowNote connects the layers</h2>
    <div class="formula">DN / NPS / A → reference size → OD + wall → calculated ID and weight</div>
    <p>The first public subset contains six Schedule 40 references. This limited scope is intentional: HowNote expands the table only after the source basis can be documented.</p>

    <div class="button-row">
      <a class="button" href="/tools/dn-nps-a-converter">Open size converter</a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/sizes">View reference table</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
